{"id":"b8612842-74b6-4ea9-9365-31b8c6a3a78d","object":"chat.completion","created":1785019700,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children_by_id = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n.get('parent')\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children_by_id[p].append(n['id'])\\n    present = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    removed_order = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in present:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required:\\n            continue\\n        if any(cid in present for cid in children_by_id.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        present.remove(nid)\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n    retained = sorted(present)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to understand the task: SE-PE-01. We have a payload with nodes (tree structure) and required_symbols. The task is to implement a \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" that reduces the tree by removing nodes, subject to constraints. The output must have removed_node_ids (list of node IDs removed, in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (list of node IDs retained, sorted).\n\nFrom the description: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" But we don't have an actual grammar or oracle; we only have the nodes and required_symbols. The description mentions \"grammar_valid\", \"interestingness_preserved\", etc. But the provided data has nodes with removable flag, parent, depth, symbol, utility. required_symbols is a list of symbols that must remain in the tree (at least one node with that symbol must be retained). We need to decide which nodes to remove.\n\nInterpretation: We have a tree of nodes. Each node has a symbol. The root cannot be removed. Nodes with removable=false cannot be removed. Nodes that are required symbols (their symbol is in required_symbols) must be retained? The description says \"Never remove a required symbol\". So nodes with symbol in required_symbols cannot be removed.\n\nWe also have a depth. \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So removal order: prioritize deeper nodes (higher depth). Among equal depth, use node ID as tie-breaker (stable). The removal order is the order in which we decide to remove nodes. That becomes the removed_node_ids list (in removal order). Retained node IDs are those not removed, sorted (presumably alphabetically or by ID string? The output just says \"array of strings\" but sorted presumably lexicographically).\n\nAlso: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So before removing a node, check that after removal, the set of symbols still covers all required_symbols. Also, we cannot remove a node that has retained children? Actually \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That means we cannot remove a node if any of its children are being retained. So we can only remove leaf nodes or nodes whose entire subtree is removed? Actually, removal of a node would remove it and all its descendants? The description says \"remove\" but doesn't specify if subtree is removed. In tree reduction, usually removing a node means removing that node and its subtree (or maybe just the node and its descendants become orphans). But the parent-child relationships: if we remove a node, its children would be removed as well. The constraint \"Never remove a node with retained children\" likely means you cannot remove a node if some of its children are still in the tree (i.e., retained). So to remove a node, all its descendants must also be removed (or be scheduled for removal). Since removal is progressive, we need to ensure that when we remove a node, all its descendants are already removed or will be removed. The phrasing \"retained children\" suggests that if any child is retained (not removed), then the parent cannot be removed. So removal order must be bottom-up: remove leaves first, then parents.\n\nGiven that we have depth, we process deepest first. At each depth, we consider removable nodes that are currently in the tree? The process: start with all nodes. We want to remove as many nodes as possible? Or we have a utility? The description doesn't mention utility for decision; it's just present. Possibly we should remove all removable nodes that are allowed? But that seems trivial. The example in the fixture? We have three cases; perhaps the expected output can be inferred? Not given. We need to deduce the algorithm.\n\nLet's read the problem statement more carefully: \"Implement `grammar_guided_node_reduction` through `solve(case)` for this bounded objective: Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" But we have no grammar or oracle. However, the data includes \"utility\". Possibly utility is used to determine which nodes to remove? Or maybe it's a hint for something else. Alternatively, it's a simplified version where we only have removable flag and required_symbols.\n\nMaybe this is a reduced version of Perses, where we try to remove nodes that are not required and whose removal doesn't break the \"interestingness\" (here, potentially that all required symbols are still present). And possibly we also have \"grammar_valid\" meaning that the tree structure must remain a valid parse tree? But we don't have grammar rules. However, we have parent relationships; removing a node would break the tree structure unless we also remove its children. So the only valid removal is to remove a leaf node (or a node whose subtree is entirely removed). So it's like a bottom-up pruning.\n\nGiven the constraints, the algorithm:\n1. Build tree: map node id to node object.\n2. Determine all nodes that are removable (removable=true) and not required (symbol not in required_symbols) and not root.\n3. Sort nodes by depth descending, then by node ID ascending (for stable tie-break).\n4. Iterate through this sorted list. For each node, check if it can be removed:\n   - The node must still be present (not already removed).\n   - All its children must already be removed (i.e., not in retained set). Since we process deepest first, this will naturally hold if we only process nodes whose descendants are already removed.\n   - After removing this node, check that all required_symbols are still represented among the remaining nodes. Since required symbols are not removable, they will still be there, but if a required symbol only appears in this node (which cannot happen because node's symbol is not required), then removal might cause loss of required symbol. But if the node is not required, its removal doesn't affect required symbols directly. However, required symbols could be in its subtree? But if the node is required, we already excluded. So no check needed? Actually, \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That implies after removal, the tree still has at least one node for each required symbol. Since the node's symbol is not required, removal alone doesn't remove a required symbol. However, if the node is a parent of a node that has a required symbol? But if the child is required, it cannot be removed. So if the child is required, it will be retained, so the parent cannot be removed because it has retained children. So the condition is automatically satisfied by the children condition. So maybe we don't need explicit required_symbols check beyond the children condition. But the problem statement says \"Never remove a required symbol\" and \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" The second is a stronger condition: even if the node itself is not required and has no retained children, its removal might affect the representation of required symbols if the only node with that required symbol becomes disconnected? But if the node's removal doesn't remove the child, then the child remains. However, if the node's removal is allowed only when children are all removed, but if a child is required, it cannot be removed, so the parent would have a retained child and thus cannot be removed. So the condition reduces to: cannot remove a node that has any child that is required, because that child will be retained. So the required_symbols check is redundant but we can still implement.\n\nThus algorithm: \n- Initialize set of present nodes = all node IDs.\n- Initialize removal_order = [].\n- Process nodes sorted by depth descending, then node ID ascending.\n- For each node:\n   - if node.id is not present: continue.\n   - if node.removable == False: continue.\n   - if node.symbol in required_symbols: continue. (but required symbols likely not removable anyway)\n   - Check if any child of node is present (i.e., child id is in present set). If yes, cannot remove because would leave retained orphans? Actually, if we remove a node, its children would become orphans. The constraint says never remove a node with retained children. So if any child is present, skip.\n   - Optional: Check that after removal, all required_symbols are still present: we can compute the set of symbols among present nodes (excluding this node's symbol? but node's symbol is not required so no effect). Actually, if the node is not required, its removal doesn't remove any required symbol. But if its removal would cause some subtree with required symbol to be removed? No, because children are not removed, they would become orphans. The constraint about retained children prevents that. So essentially no extra check.\n   - Then remove the node: remove its ID from present set, add to removal_order.\n- After processing all, retained = sorted list of remaining present nodes (by ID string).\n- removed_node_ids = removal_order (list in order of removal).\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained}.\n\nBut note: The removal_order is the order in which we remove nodes. Since we process deepest first, and we only remove nodes whose children are all removed, the removal order will be bottom-up. That matches \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" Good.\n\nEdge cases: The root (id=\"root\") has removable=false and its symbol is \"root\" which is in required_symbols presumably. So root cannot be removed.\n\nTest with the first case:\n- All nodes: root (depth0, non-removable, required), n1 (depth1, non-removable), n2 (depth1, removable), n3 (depth1, non-removable), n4 (depth2, removable), n5 (depth2, non-removable), n6 (depth2, removable). required_symbols: root, s1. Symbols: root: root, n1:s1 (required), n2:s2, n3:s0, n4:s1 (required), n5:s2, n6:s0.\nRemovable nodes: n2 (s2), n4 (s1 but s1 required), n6 (s0). So n4 is required, cannot remove. So only n2 and n6 are candidates. But n6 is leaf, depth2, n2 is depth1. Process depth2 first: n6 (leaf, removable, not required) -> check children: none. Remove n6. Then depth2 nodes: n4 is not removable (required), n5 non-removable. Then depth1: n2 (removable, not required). Check children: n4 is still present (since n4 not removed). So n2 has retained child n4 (n4 is required, so retained). Cannot remove n2. So removal_order = [\"n6\"]. Retained: all except n6. But wait, n6 is removable and not required, so it's removed. So retained: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. Sorted: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]. But root should be first? Sorted string: \"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\" - lexicographically, \"n1\" < \"n2\" < ... < \"root\"? Actually \"root\" comes after \"n5\"? Compare: 'n' vs 'r', 'n' < 'r', so \"root\" will be last. That seems plausible.\n\nBut is this the expected output? Without ground truth, we need to implement as described. The problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs\". It doesn't specify ascending order but typically sorted lexicographically. So we'll sort.\n\nOne nuance: When we remove a node, should we also remove its descendants? The description says \"remove\" but doesn't elaborate. In tree reduction, if you remove a node, the subtree is gone. Our algorithm only removes the node itself, but if its children are still present, they become orphaned. The constraint \"never remove a node with retained children\" prevents that. So we must ensure that children are removed before parent. Our processing order (deepest first) will ensure that if we remove children first, then parent becomes childless and can be removed. So in the above, n2 cannot be removed because child n4 hasn't been removed (n4 cannot be removed). So that's fine.\n\nBut consider a scenario where a node has children that are also removable and not required. Then we would remove children first based on order. So algorithm works.\n\nThus implementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    # Build lookup by id\n    node_by_id = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\n    # Build children mapping\n    children_by_id = {node['id']: [] for node in nodes}\n    for node in nodes:\n        parent = node.get('parent')\n        if parent is not None:\n            children_by_id[parent].append(node['id'])\n    # Get list of all node ids\n    all_ids = set(node_by_id.keys())\n    present = set(all_ids)  # current retained set\n    removed_order = []\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending for stable tie\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid not in present:\n            continue\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if node['symbol'] in required:\n            continue  # never remove required symbol\n        # Check that all children are already removed (i.e., not present)\n        children = children_by_id.get(nid, [])\n        if any(cid in present for cid in children):\n            continue\n        # Optionally check required symbols still represented: the only potential issue is if the node's removal would remove the only node with a required symbol. Since node is not required, and its children are removed, no required symbol is lost from the tree. But we can be safe: compute symbols in present minus this node's symbol (if this node had any required symbol, which it doesn't). So it's fine.\n        # Remove the node\n        present.remove(nid)\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n    # Retained ids sorted\n    retained = sorted(present)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained}\n\nCheck second case: similar structure with different utilities but same removable flags and required symbols. So n4 is still required (symbol s1) so cannot be removed. n6 removable, n2 removable but child n4 present. So same result: remove n6 only. Third case: similar.\n\nBut wait: The first case's n4 has symbol s1 and removable=true, but required_symbols includes s1, so we say never remove required symbol, so n4 is not considered. That matches.\n\nBut what about nodes that are removable and whose children are all removable? For example, if there was a node with two children both removable and not required. Then we would remove children (depth deeper) first, then parent. That is correct.\n\nThus the algorithm seems to produce consistent results.\n\nWe need to output the Python source as a string in JSON. So we'll write the implementation inside a JSON object with key \"implementation\". Ensure escaping.\n\nAlso note: The case argument is the payload object directly, as per instruction: \"The argument is exactly the inner object stored in that row's payload field.\" So function signature def solve(case): and case is the payload dict.\n\nWe'll write the code accordingly.\n\nPotential edge: There might be nodes that are not in the children mapping if they have no children. That's fine.\n\nAlso, we must not mutate case. Our code does not mutate case; we create new structures.\n\nReturn value is a dict that matches the structural schema.\n\nNow write the full response as a JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a string of Python source.\n\nWe'll produce a clean code.\n\nLet's write the source."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2057,"completion_tokens":3662,"total_tokens":5719,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3387},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":9},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}